# The slice

`TimesheetEntry` → `TimesheetProvider` (4 methods) → `TimesheetController` (`TimesheetGrid`,
`TimesheetGet`, `TimesheetSave`, `TimesheetListSave`, `TimesheetDelete`) → `~TimesheetService.ts` →
`~TimesheetController.ts`, an editable grid with a daily-hours summary.

# Hazards

**Date handling is fragile and split across four layers.** The provider zeroes the time with
`dateEntry.AddHours(dateEntry.Hour * -1)` to undo a UTC offset added by the UI; the Angular service
converts the grid's start/end to UTC; `TimesheetSave` applies `.ToLocalTime()` but `TimesheetListSave`
does **not**; and `onLoadEvent` appends a computed timezone suffix to every `dateEntry` string after
load. Change one layer and verify the round trip end to end — a saved date shifting by a day is the
expected failure mode.

**The billing-cycle-closed guard is duplicated three times** in `TimesheetProvider` (insert, update,
delete) and each copy enumerates the whole `BillingCycleEntrySet` client-side. Any new write path must
repeat the guard or closed periods become editable. Prefer extracting it to one private method.

**Delete is a hard delete.** `DeleteTimesheetEntry` calls `Remove(record)` with the
`record.IsActive = false` line commented out, even though reads filter on `IsActive`. Confirm intent
before relying on either behaviour.

**Row-level access is enforced by the caller.** `TimesheetFilterList(startDate, endDate)` returns *all*
users' entries; only `TimesheetGrid` narrows it with `.Where(a => a.UserAccountId == model.UserId)`. New
read paths must filter explicitly. The two relevant privileges are `TimesheetCapture` and
`TimesheetCaptureForOtherAccounts`, and the provider only checks that the user has *either*, including
for delete.

**`TimesheetGrid` returns every matching row** — its `Skip`/`Take` is commented out and the grid pages
client-side.

**Hours are quantized to quarter-hours in the browser only** (`validateHours`); the API accepts any
decimal. Add server-side validation if it matters.

# Cost of a new field

Adding one field to a timesheet entry touches roughly ten places: entity, migration,
`SaveTimesheetEntry` (already 9 positional parameters — consider a parameter object),
`ITimesheetProvider`, three separate mapping blocks in the controller, `TimesheetModel`,
`TimesheetGridModel`, `~Models.ts`, `mainView.html`, and a `case` in the sort switch.
